1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,535
Myopia and eye development.

2
00:00:02,535 --> 00:00:05,538
When babies are born,
their eyes are hyperopic,

3
00:00:05,672 --> 00:00:10,010
or farsighted, meaning that their
eyes are too short front-to-back.

4
00:00:10,110 --> 00:00:13,513
This means light focuses
behind the retina, making

5
00:00:13,513 --> 00:00:15,181
close up images blurry. 

6
00:00:15,181 --> 00:00:17,584
By age 4, most children's eyes

7
00:00:17,584 --> 00:00:21,554
naturally start to elongate,
correcting the hyperopia.

8
00:00:21,654 --> 00:00:25,458
However, in some children, this eye
growth either doesn't stop,

9
00:00:25,558 --> 00:00:28,561
or it restarts, leading to myopia.

10
00:00:28,928 --> 00:00:32,532
Myopia development usually begins
when children are school-age.

11
00:00:32,532 --> 00:00:36,369
Rather than growing proportionally,
the developing eye starts

12
00:00:36,369 --> 00:00:38,071
to grow too long.

13
00:00:38,071 --> 00:00:41,674
This usually progresses until
the eye reaches its adult size.

14
00:00:42,008 --> 00:00:45,445
In a myopic eye, light
focuses in front of the retina,

15
00:00:45,445 --> 00:00:47,414
making faraway images blurry.

16
00:00:47,414 --> 00:00:50,950
Blurry distance
vision can be corrected with lenses

17
00:00:51,117 --> 00:00:53,119
which refract light entering the eye

18
00:00:53,119 --> 00:00:55,789
to refocus it properly
on the central retina.

19
00:00:56,122 --> 00:01:00,093
However, regular glasses
do not slow myopia development.

20
00:01:00,593 --> 00:01:03,229
High myopia can lead to blinding
conditions

21
00:01:03,229 --> 00:01:06,232
like retinal detachment in later
life.

22
00:01:06,332 --> 00:01:09,969
Researchers are exploring ways
to slow myopia progression

23
00:01:09,969 --> 00:01:10,904
in children.

24
00:01:11,137 --> 00:01:12,872
National Eye Institute.

25
00:01:12,872 --> 00:01:17,577
Learn more about
myopia at nei.nih.gov/myopia.